::ALG1_U04_L01_GP_Q01::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1] Which relationship is a function? {
~A. (1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 4) #Input 1 has two different outputs, so this is not a function.
=B. (1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 4) #Correct. Each input has exactly one output.
~C. (4, 1), (4, 2), (5, 3) #Input 4 has two different outputs.
~D. (2, 5), (2, 5), (2, 6) #Input 2 is paired with different outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_GP_Q02::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1] Why is (2, 8), (2, 9), (3, 10) not a function? {
~A. It has repeated outputs #The outputs are not repeated; the issue is the repeated input.
~B. It has only three ordered pairs #A function can have three ordered pairs.
~C. It includes the input 3 #The input 3 is not a problem.
=D. Input 2 has two different outputs #Correct. A function cannot assign one input to two different outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_GP_Q03::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1] Can different inputs have the same output in a function? {
=A. Yes, because the rule is about each input having one output #Correct. Repeated outputs are allowed.
~B. Yes, but only for linear functions #Repeated outputs can occur in different function types.
~C. No, repeated outputs always fail the function test #Repeated inputs with different outputs fail the test, not repeated outputs.
~D. No, every output must be unique #Functions do not require unique outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_GP_Q04::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1] What does it mean if a vertical line crosses a graph twice? {
~A. The graph is linear #Linearity is not determined by one vertical line crossing twice.
~B. The graph has repeated outputs #A vertical line checks repeated inputs, not repeated outputs.
=C. The graph is not a function #Correct. One input has more than one output.
~D. The graph must have slope 0 #A horizontal line has slope 0 and is still a function.
}

::ALG1_U04_L01_GP_Q05::[MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1] Which type best describes y = 3x + 2? {
~A. Quadratic #A quadratic function includes an x^2 term.
=B. Linear #Correct. The equation has the form y = mx + b.
~C. Exponential #An exponential function has the variable in the exponent.
~D. Absolute value #An absolute value function includes an absolute value expression.
}
